WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.913
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.454 --> 00:00:11.486
Can we talk about how you evaluate whether
someone will engage academically at

00:00:11.486 --> 00:00:12.104
Stanford?

00:00:12.104 --> 00:00:15.983
&gt;&gt; We're looking for students who are
curious, who are willing to share their

00:00:15.983 --> 00:00:19.560
perspectives both inside and
outside of the classroom.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.680
And perhaps most importantly,
we're looking for

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.720
students who want to seek new ideas,
new perspectives,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.020
almost insights or truth, and
to do that in a collaborative way.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.080
When you leave JSB as a leader,
you're going to go out and

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.670
have some tough problems to solve.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.170
But you're not going to do it by yourself,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.100
you're not going to do it
in your own head somewhere.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.570
So really having someone who's going to be
leveraging the thoughts of others to come

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.660
up with the best possible
solution is important.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.400
To assess these behaviors, we look at
multiple places on the application.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.640
The most obvious would be the transcript.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.020
And for me, I usually read,
I'll take a day, Tuesdays and

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.940
Thursdays during application season,
I get to read from home.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.912
It's my favorite thing to do,
and every time-

00:00:59.912 --> 00:01:00.511
&gt;&gt; It sounds fun.

00:01:00.511 --> 00:01:03.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It is, I get really along
with the applicants and their stories.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.171
But when I open a file, one of the first
things I do is I go to the transcript,

00:01:07.171 --> 00:01:10.990
because that tells so much about someone,
what are the kind of courses you took?

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.955
What were you most interested in studying?

00:01:12.955 --> 00:01:16.730
Are you someone who went broad and
liked to see how different

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:20.340
disciplines connect with each other,
or maybe someone who went very deep?

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.690
So, that's step one, and I do get
information about how curious someone is,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:29.650
and how the rigor and
course choices impacted them.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:33.375
But perhaps more importantly than
the transcript is looking for

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:37.265
evidence of this curiosity and
other things that people have done.

00:01:37.265 --> 00:01:40.935
So perhaps you were at work and
there's a tough problems to solve, and

00:01:40.935 --> 00:01:42.705
you worked with others to figure it out.

00:01:42.705 --> 00:01:46.195
Or perhaps you did research at your
university beyond your classes.

00:01:46.195 --> 00:01:51.465
One of my most recent favorite stories was
from someone we had met in this class,

00:01:51.465 --> 00:01:52.345
it was an individual.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:55.770
I think he was in business
development in a tech company,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.230
it was a growing tech company.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.930
And he joined when the company
was only about 50 people.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.110
And he said one of the things he loved
the best when he joined was that,

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:05.840
wherever he was there was
laughter in the hallways.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.940
And he thought that was a sign of
people being optimistic, innovative.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.960
And then as time went on,
and they were doubling, and

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.680
tripling in size, the laughter went away.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:15.330
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and he remembered going
to an all-hands meeting, and

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.520
asked the senior management,
this is a concern, what can I do?

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.250
And came up with the idea
to do a pulse survey.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.350
The only problem to check
in with the culture,

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.780
the only problem was he said he had
no idea how to do an HR survey.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.840
What cultural engagement was?

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.521
How to build it?

00:02:33.521 --> 00:02:35.710
How to write questions, all of that.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:39.220
But you watch them go through a path of
just curiosity of meeting with experts,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.750
both in the company and
outside of the company.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:45.160
Researching material to figure out, yeah,
what are some good benchmark surveys?

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.520
How do we develop
questions that will work?

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.877
Luckily, they had some PhD students in
this company who were doing business

00:02:50.877 --> 00:02:52.562
analytics that I could get help from.

00:02:52.562 --> 00:02:57.549
But the point is, that's a great
story about using your curiosity, and

00:02:57.549 --> 00:03:00.674
your drive to find truth
about this company.

00:03:01.794 --> 00:03:04.754
They essentially came up with a great
answer about what they could be doing

00:03:04.754 --> 00:03:05.974
differently as they move forward.

00:03:05.974 --> 00:03:09.364
So, hopefully that helps give you
a sense of what I'm looking for and

00:03:09.364 --> 00:03:10.104
how I'm looking for.

